'''''Mentha'' × ''villosa''''' (syn: '''''Mentha alopecuroides''''', '''''Mentha nemorosa''''', '''''Mentha villosa''''' var. '''''alopecuroides''''') also known as '''hairy mint''' or '''mojito mint''' is a [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] species of [[Mentha|mint]], a cross between ''[[Mentha spicata]]'' and ''[[Mentha suaveolens]]''.<ref>Kew Plants of the World Online: [https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:451312-1 ''Mentha'' × ''villosa'' Huds.]</ref><ref>{{GRIN|sp|313220|07-Oct-06}}</ref>

This species is native to [[temperate]] and warm temperate regions of Europe and occurs in meadows, pastures, and [[ruderal]] locations.<ref name="jager-etal">Eckehart J. Jäger, Friedrich Ebel, Peter Hanelt, Gerd K. Müller (eds.) (2008). ''Rothmaler: Exkursionsflora von Deutschland. Band 5: Krautige Zier- und Nutzpflanzen''. Berlin: Spektrum Akademischer Verlag. {{ISBN|978-3-8274-0918-8}}. p. 496.</ref> However, it is cultivated in many other countries throughout the world.

In [[Cuba]] and the [[Philippines]], this species is known as [[yerba buena]] or hierbabuena. In Cuba, it is a core ingredient in the [[mojito]] cocktail, though other mints such as [[spearmint]] are used where mojito mint is not available.<ref>"[https://books.google.com/books?id=XkKS0-dGllgC&lpg=PP1&ots=yIQ_ZQIKmk&pg=PA12#v=onepage&q&f=false Mojito (Rum Spritzer)]" from ''Miami Spice: The New Florida Cuisine'' by Steven Raichlen, 1993. {{ISBN|0761164391}}. p. 12</ref><ref>"[https://books.google.com/books?id=R9i5BgAAQBAJ&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&lpg=PT1840&pg=PT1840#v=onepage&q&f=false Rum Cocktails]" by Bill Kte’pi. In: Scott C. Martin (ed) (2015). ''The SAGE Encyclopedia of Alcohol: Social, Cultural, and Historical Perspectives''. Vol 2, p. 1082-1084.</ref><ref>"[https://web.archive.org/web/20220328060526/https://cookmundo.com/mojito/ Original Cuban Mojito: Hierbabuena or Spearmint?]" by John Kiely, ''Cookmundo'' (blog), 28 April, 2018. (Archived from original 28 March, 2022 at [[Archive.org]])</ref> In the Philippines, this species has a long history of medicinal use and is one of 10 medicinal plant species that the Philippine government has endorsed as effective.<ref name="10.47895_amp.vi0.8401">Rodriguez JP, Tolosa EN, Legaspi CLB. (2023). "[https://actamedicaphilippina.upm.edu.ph/index.php/acta/article/view/8401/5290 Pharmacologic Activities of Yerba Buena (''Mentha'' x ''villosa'' Huds. Fam. Lamiaceae): An Overview]." ''Acta Medica Philippina'': Early Access 2023. {{doi|10.47895/amp.vi0.8401}}</ref>

==Description==

''Mentha'' × ''villosa'' is a [[herbaceous]], rhizomatous, [[perennial plant]] that grows to be {{convert|30|-|60|cm|in|abbr=on}} tall, with smooth stems, square in cross section. The [[rhizome]]s are wide-spreading and fleshy, and bear fibrous [[root]]s.

==Cultivation==
Historically, this species has been included in European medicinal and aromatic plant gardens, though its cultivation in Europe is in decline.<ref name="jager-etal"/> However, the mojito mint variety from Cuba has become increasingly popular in the United States and elsewhere as a culinary garden plant for making [[mojito]]s and other culinary uses.<ref>[https://www.joyusgarden.com/grow-mojito-mint/ "Grow Mojito Mint" ''JoyusGarden.com''](accessed Nov. 29, 2022)</ref> It is a perennial and grows primarily in the temperate biome.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mentha × villosa Huds. {{!}} Plants of the World Online {{!}} Kew Science |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:451312-1 |access-date=2025-01-09 |website=Plants of the World Online |language=en}}</ref>

